    "pros": [
      "33 in width and 6 in thickness for stability",
      "Complete kit in one box",
      "15 to 18 PSI recommended for a stiffer board",
      "Bluefin gives clear size guidance"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Rider limit listed as 287 or 330 lb depending on year",
      "Board weight varies widely between model years",
      "Carbon version specs conflict between sources"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A complete, stable kit for beginners, once you confirm the model year's rider limit."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Paddle Boards",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-paddle-board/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best paddle board for beginners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A wide, stable inflatable all-rounder around 10 ft 6 in to 11 ft long and 33 to 34 in wide is the best start. The Isle Pioneer 3 and BOTE Breeze Aero are both stable, light and sold as complete kits. Make sure the board's capacity is comfortably above your weight."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size paddle board do I need for my weight?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use the board's listed capacity as the first filter and stay well inside it. BOTE suggests choosing a larger board if you are within 50 lb of the listed capacity. Lighter adults can use boards rated around 250 to 290 lb, while heavier riders should look at boards rated 315 lb or more, such as the Atoll 11 or Gili Meno."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are inflatable paddle boards good?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for most recreational paddlers. A well-made inflatable at the correct pressure feels firm, packs into a bag and resists dings far better than a hard board. Hard boards still glide better and suit surfing and racing, but inflatables are easier to store, transport and live with."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What PSI should I inflate my paddle board to?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Follow the manufacturer's recommended range, which for the boards here runs from about 10 to 18 PSI. BOTE lists 10 to 15 PSI for the Breeze Aero, and Bluefin recommends 15 to 18 PSI for the Cruise. Inflate toward the top of the range for heavier riders and never exceed the maximum."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a life jacket on a paddle board?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The US Coast Guard classifies paddle boards used outside swimming, surfing and bathing areas as vessels, so adults must carry a Coast Guard-approved life jacket and a whistle, and many states require children to wear one. Wearing a PFD is the safest choice, especially in cold water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How heavy is an inflatable paddle board?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The boards here are listed at roughly 19 to 25 lb, with full kits including paddle, pump and bag weighing about 26 to 38 lb. Board weight varies between model years, so check the listing for the version you are buying, and choose a wheeled bag if you walk far to the water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can two people use one paddle board?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if the board's capacity covers both riders plus anything they carry, and most all-round boards are sized for one adult. A high-capacity board such as the Gili Meno, listed at 450 lb, can carry a paddler with a child or a dog, or two lighter riders, on calm water."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Outdoor Gear",
    tags: [
      "best paddle board",
      "inflatable paddle board",
      "best inflatable sup",
      "paddle board for beginners",
      "best stand up paddle board"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Paddle Boards</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Stable inflatable all-rounders for first-timers, a high-capacity board for bigger riders, an 11 ft glider and a premium pick, with sizing and PSI explained.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#isle-pioneer-3"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#inflatable-vs-hard" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Inflatable vs Hard Boards</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing and Capacity</a>
          <a href="#inflation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Inflation and Pumps</a>
          <a href="#kit" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. What Comes in the Kit</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Safety on the Water</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Care and Storage</a>
          <a href="#budget" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. How Much to Spend</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most paddlers, the Isle Pioneer 3 is the paddle board to buy: a 34 in wide, 10 ft 6 in inflatable at around 19 lb, sold with a carbon paddle and a wheeled bag. Heavier riders should start with the Gili Meno and its listed 450 lb capacity, and value hunters should look at the Atoll 11. Size the board to your weight first; everything else comes second.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good paddle board for most people is stable enough to stand on without a fight, light enough to carry to the water, sized for the rider's weight and sold with a kit that does not need upgrading on day one. Speed and touring performance matter far less for most buyers than whether they feel confident standing up on the first outing.
          </p>

          <p>
            We built this list from manufacturer specifications, retailer listings and published reviews, and on each pick we note where sources disagree. We did not lab-test these boards. Board weight, capacity and width are the figures most often misreported between model years, so we flag the conflicts rather than pick one number at random.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick is an inflatable all-round board. That reflects what most people buy and why: inflatables store in a closet, travel in a car boot and survive knocks against rocks and docks far better than a hard board. All-round shapes, roughly 10 to 11 ft long and 32 to 34 in wide, are the most forgiving and versatile.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted stability, capacity, weight and kit quality above everything else. If you already paddle a <a href="/best-canoes-for-river-paddling/">canoe</a> or kayak, a board is a different experience, closer to walking on water than sitting in a boat, and a forgiving all-round shape is the right place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inflatable-vs-hard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Inflatable vs Hard Boards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hard boards, made from epoxy over a foam core, are stiffer, glide better and respond more precisely to each stroke. They are the choice for surfing and serious racing, and some paddlers simply prefer the feel. They are also heavy, awkward to transport and store, and easy to ding against rocks or a concrete dock.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inflatable boards use drop-stitch construction, in which thousands of threads hold the top and bottom layers a fixed distance apart, so the board becomes rigid when inflated to its rated pressure. A well-made inflatable at the right pressure feels surprisingly firm, packs into a backpack and shrugs off impacts that would crack a hard board.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most recreational paddlers on lakes, calm bays and slow rivers, an inflatable is the better buy. It is easier to store, easier to carry to the water, easier to take on holiday and more forgiving of the bumps that come with learning. The small loss in glide matters little at recreational speeds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a hard board if you have storage space and a roof rack, plan to surf or race, or paddle often enough that a little extra performance is worth the inconvenience. Otherwise, an inflatable all-rounder will be the board you actually use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 7 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We researched these from manufacturer specifications, retailer listings and published reviews, and we note where sources disagree, which is common for board weight and rider capacity between model years. We did not lab-test them.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
                        width="40"
