        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: Length, Width, Thickness and Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Capacity is the first filter. Each board has a listed maximum rider weight, and staying well inside it keeps the board riding high and stable. BOTE's advice is a useful rule of thumb: if your weight is within 50 lb of a board's listed capacity, choose a larger board. A 200 lb paddler, for example, should look at boards rated around 250 lb or more, and higher still if carrying a dog or a cooler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width governs stability. Boards around 32 in wide suit most adults with some balance; 33 to 34 in boards feel noticeably steadier for beginners, heavier riders and anyone planning to fish or do yoga. Wider boards are slower, but at recreational speeds most people will not notice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Length governs glide and tracking. Boards around 10 ft 6 in turn easily and suit beginners and short outings. Boards around 11 ft glide further per stroke and hold a straighter line, which makes them better for longer paddles. Touring boards of 12 ft 6 in and more are faster still but narrower and less forgiving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thickness matters for inflatables. Most all-round inflatables are 6 in thick, which adds buoyancy and stiffness and suits heavier riders. Some premium boards, such as the Red Paddle Co Ride at about 4.7 in, are thinner, which puts you closer to the water and can feel more stable for lighter riders but offers less buoyancy for heavier ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inflation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Inflation, PSI and Pumps
          </h2>

          <p>
            An inflatable board is only as stiff as its pressure. Manufacturers list a recommended range, such as 10 to 15 PSI for the BOTE Breeze Aero and 15 to 18 PSI for the Bluefin Cruise, and a board run below that range flexes in the middle, which makes it slower and less stable. Inflate to at least the bottom of the recommended range, and closer to the top for heavier riders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hand pumps are included with every board here, and a dual-action pump that pushes air on both strokes makes the job far quicker. Expect the last few PSI to take real effort, since the pump gets harder to push as pressure rises. Many paddlers find it easiest to pump in two stages, resting between.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric pumps that plug into a car's 12 V socket take the work out of inflation and can be set to stop automatically at a chosen pressure. If you paddle away from the car or want to run one at a campsite, a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a> with a 12 V outlet can run most SUP pumps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check pressure before each outing and after the board has sat in hot sun, since heat raises pressure and cold lowers it. Never exceed the board's maximum rated pressure, and release a little air if the board will sit in direct sun for a long time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Comes in the Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most inflatable boards are sold as kits that include a paddle, pump, leash, fin and bag, and the quality of those parts varies far more than the boards themselves. The paddle is the item most worth checking: a lighter carbon or fibreglass paddle, such as the carbon paddle in the Isle Pioneer kit, is far less tiring than a heavy aluminium shaft over an hour of paddling.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bag matters if you walk any distance to the water. Wheeled bags, such as Isle's, are far easier than backpacks for long walks from the car park. Packed kit weights here run from about 26 lb for the BOTE to about 38 lb for the Atoll, so a wheeled bag can make a real difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fins come in two main layouts: a single large removable center fin, which tracks well, and a 2+1 setup with two small side fins as well. Removable center fins are easy to swap or remove for shallow water. Always fit the fin before launching; paddling without one makes the board spin with every stroke.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leashes connect you to the board so it does not drift away if you fall in. A coiled leash suits flatwater because it does not drag, while a quick-release waist leash is the safer choice on moving water, where a leash snagged on an underwater obstacle can be dangerous.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety on the Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            The US Coast Guard classifies paddle boards used outside swimming, surfing and bathing areas as vessels, which means adults must carry a Coast Guard-approved life jacket and a sound-producing device such as a whistle, and children must wear a life jacket under many state rules. Belt-pack inflatable PFDs are popular with paddlers because they are unobtrusive, but they must be inflated after you enter the water, so many beginners prefer a wearable vest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear a leash in almost all conditions. Inflatable boards are light and catch the wind easily, and a board blown away from you can drift faster than you can swim. On rivers and in current, use a quick-release leash worn at the waist rather than the ankle, so you can release it if it snags.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect the wind. Paddle boards are high-sided targets, and an offshore wind can make the return trip much harder than the outbound one. Start out into the wind so the trip home is downwind, check the forecast and stay close to shore until you know how the board handles in a breeze.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold water is a serious hazard even on sunny days. Dress for the water temperature rather than the air, and practise climbing back onto the board from the water near shore. Kneeling is a perfectly good way to paddle in chop or wind, and dropping to your knees is often the safest response when conditions change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Care and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            After each outing, rinse the board, fin and leash with fresh water, especially after saltwater or muddy lakes, and let everything dry before packing. A <a href="/best-garden-hose/">garden hose</a> with a gentle <a href="/best-garden-hose-nozzle/">spray nozzle</a> does the job quickly; avoid high-pressure jets on seams and valves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dry the board completely before rolling it, since trapped moisture can cause mildew in the bag. Roll it from the nose toward the valve end so air escapes, and avoid tight folds along the same lines every time. Store it in a cool, dry place out of direct sun, and keep it away from sharp objects.
          </p>

          <p>
            UV exposure is the main long-term enemy of inflatable boards. Leaving a board on a hot dock or beach for hours can weaken the PVC and seams. Shade it between sessions, and consider a UV-protectant spray designed for inflatables if it spends long days outside.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you keep the board inflated through the season, store it slightly below full pressure and out of the sun. Check the valve and seams at the start of each season, keep the repair kit in your bag, and fix small leaks promptly before they spread.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much to Spend
          </h2>

          <p>
            Inflatable boards run from budget kits under a few hundred dollars to premium boards that cost several times as much. The cheapest boards often save money on the paddle, pump and drop-stitch construction, and the result is a board that flexes and a paddle that tires you out. A mid-range kit is usually the better buy for anyone planning to paddle regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mid-range kits, such as the Atoll 11 and the Bluefin Cruise, give you a stable board and a complete kit at a moderate price. Premium boards, such as the Red Paddle Co Ride, cost more for stiffer construction, better finish and longer life rather than for more capacity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you spend, prioritise capacity and width for your weight over brand name. A premium board that is too small for you will feel worse than a mid-range board sized correctly. If you are heavier or plan to paddle with a dog or a child, put capacity first and choose a high-capacity board such as the Gili Meno.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you later want to fish from the water, a dedicated <a href="/best-fishing-kayak/">fishing kayak</a> offers far more stability, storage and rigging space than any paddle board. Many paddlers end up owning both: a board for exercise and casual outings and a kayak for fishing trips.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a board too small for your weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Riding close to a board's listed capacity makes it sit low, flex and feel unstable. BOTE's rule of thumb is to size up if you are within 50 lb of the capacity. Add the weight of anything you carry, such as a dog, a cooler or a child, before choosing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Under-inflating the board</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An inflatable below its recommended pressure flexes in the middle, which makes it slower and wobblier. Inflate to at least the bottom of the listed range, closer to the top for heavier riders, and check pressure again if the board has sat in the sun or cold.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the leash</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Inflatable boards are light and catch the wind, and a board blown away from you can drift faster than you can swim. Wear a coiled leash on flatwater, and a quick-release waist leash on rivers, where an ankle leash snagged on an obstacle can be dangerous.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paddling out with the wind behind you</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An offshore or following wind makes the outbound trip easy and the return a struggle. Start into the wind so the ride home is downwind, check the forecast and stay close to shore until you know how the board handles in a breeze. Kneel if conditions get rough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it wet or in the sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rolling a wet board can trap moisture and cause mildew, and long UV exposure weakens PVC and seams. Rinse with fresh water, dry the board completely before packing, and store it in a cool, shaded place. Shade it between sessions on hot days.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

