        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best paddle board for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A wide, stable inflatable all-rounder around 10 ft 6 in to 11 ft long and 33 to 34 in wide is the best start. The Isle Pioneer 3 and BOTE Breeze Aero are both stable, light and sold as complete kits. Make sure the board's capacity is comfortably above your weight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size paddle board do I need for my weight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the board's listed capacity as the first filter and stay well inside it. BOTE suggests choosing a larger board if you are within 50 lb of the listed capacity. Lighter adults can use boards rated around 250 to 290 lb, while heavier riders should look at boards rated 315 lb or more, such as the Atoll 11 or Gili Meno.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are inflatable paddle boards good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for most recreational paddlers. A well-made inflatable at the correct pressure feels firm, packs into a bag and resists dings far better than a hard board. Hard boards still glide better and suit surfing and racing, but inflatables are easier to store, transport and live with.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What PSI should I inflate my paddle board to?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the manufacturer's recommended range, which for the boards here runs from about 10 to 18 PSI. BOTE lists 10 to 15 PSI for the Breeze Aero, and Bluefin recommends 15 to 18 PSI for the Cruise. Inflate toward the top of the range for heavier riders and never exceed the maximum.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a life jacket on a paddle board?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The US Coast Guard classifies paddle boards used outside swimming, surfing and bathing areas as vessels, so adults must carry a Coast Guard-approved life jacket and a whistle, and many states require children to wear one. Wearing a PFD is the safest choice, especially in cold water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How heavy is an inflatable paddle board?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The boards here are listed at roughly 19 to 25 lb, with full kits including paddle, pump and bag weighing about 26 to 38 lb. Board weight varies between model years, so check the listing for the version you are buying, and choose a wheeled bag if you walk far to the water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can two people use one paddle board?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the board's capacity covers both riders plus anything they carry, and most all-round boards are sized for one adult. A high-capacity board such as the Gili Meno, listed at 450 lb, can carry a paddler with a child or a dog, or two lighter riders, on calm water.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

